				28,
				1
			],
			"Message": "fix(Sky): Pin @vscode/vscode-languagedetection version to 1.0.23\n\nUpdate the Sky submodule to pin the `@vscode/vscode-languagedetection` dependency to version 1.0.23. This resolves potential compatibility issues and ensures deterministic builds by preventing accidental upgrades to newer, potentially unstable versions of the language detection model.\n\nThe editor's language identification capabilities remain consistent across development and production environments.",
			"SHA": "77c628ad4ef538b8b6913d76ee4b5102dfbbecd6"
		},
		{
			"Author": "Nikola Hristov",
			"Date": "2026-05-03T09:34:21Z",
			"File": [
				"⡿⠀⠀⠀⠀",
				28,
				5
			],
			"Message": "chore(Element): Synchronize 5 submodules for build consistency, path normalization, and artifact refresh\n\nUpdate five Element submodules to resolve critical build pipeline inconsistencies, fix macOS-specific path resolution, and refresh native artifacts.\n\nCocoon migrates ESBuild configurations to strict PascalCase naming, introducing explicit constants for dev/prod builds and disabling minification in favor of unminified debug artifacts before re-applying aggressive production minification. Mountain generalizes hardcoded volume paths in documentation to ensure environment-agnostic portability. Output reformats its entire ESBuild pipeline and polyfills for readability before applying final minification and removing source maps to reduce bundle size. Rest regenerates its Rust binary to match the latest telemetry and configuration updates. Wind fixes a macOS APFS path mismatch where trailing slashes in workspace URIs caused breadcrumb rendering failures by normalizing URI resolution.\n\nThese changes ensure the extension host is debuggable yet optimized, the native backend is portable, and the editor correctly handles file paths on macOS.",
			"SHA": "bc686ceeea5ab6ef1a7e75ad968c13ffcecf14bd"
		},
		{
			"Author": "Nikola Hristov",
			"Date": "2026-05-03T09:34:17Z",
			"File": [
				"⠁⠀⠀⠀⠀",
				28,
				1
			],
			"Message": "feat(Mountain): Synchronize v2.2 IPC security, telemetry, and service handlers\n\nUpdate the Mountain backend submodule to implement the v2.2 correctness pass, introducing a comprehensive security and observability layer alongside new native service handlers. The update replaces implicit trust with a strict Role-Based Access Control (RBAC) engine, enforcing default-deny policies via `SecurityContext` and a configurable `Validator`. Simultaneously, integrate a cryptographically secure IPC channel using AES-256-GCM with automatic key rotation and replay protection.\n\nOperational capabilities are expanded with new Tauri command handlers for Source Control Management, Terminal lifecycle, Text Model management, Extension registry, and persistent Storage. A distributed tracing system is established via the `PerformanceDashboard` module, collecting system metrics and managing trace spans with W3C `traceparent` injection for end-to-end visibility. The codebase strictly adheres to the project's modular 'one struct per file' architecture and PascalCase naming conventions, decomposing monolithic IPC handlers into discrete units for improved testability and symbol resolution.\n\nThese changes solidify the native backend infrastructure, enabling secure, observable, and feature-parity interactions between the Rust substrate and the TypeScript workbench.",
			"SHA": "38a4c21ef5399bbf53277a509850bb31176ceee2"
		},
		{
			"Author": "Nikola Hristov",
			"Date": "2026-05-03T08:09:33Z",
			"File": [
				"⣿⣿⡿⠀⠀",
				28,
				16
			],
			"Message": "chore(Element): Synchronize 14 submodules to establish fleet-wide telemetry and finalize Tauri migration\n\nSynchronize 14 Element submodules to unify observability across the sidecar ecosystem and complete the Tauri IPC substrate. Common introduces the shared dual-pipe telemetry module (PostHog + OTLP) with W3C traceparent support, adopted by Air, Echo, Grove, Mist, Rest, SideCar, and Worker to enable end-to-end distributed tracing. Cocoon integrates OTLP bridges with production-safe gating, while Wind finalizes the workbench communication layer by implementing concrete Effect-TS service layers and replacing 1,076 lines of legacy Electron polyfills. Sky adds event replay mechanisms to prevent data loss during startup and enforces telemetry kill-switches. Output and Maintain undergo formatting standardization to align with project readability and rustfmt conventions. This synchronization establishes a consistent observability surface, resolves boot-time event loss, and solidifies the native backend infrastructure for the editor.",
			"SHA": "36f5b22811ad80a0299e8243e3f7aaea8f5d4517"
		}
